1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:29,980
We'll be right back.

2
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:59,980
We'll be right back.

3
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:02,620
I'm also in shock that it's here already.

4
00:01:02,620 --> 00:01:05,360
So if we haven't met yet, my name is Ryan.

5
00:01:05,360 --> 00:01:07,340
I have the privilege of being one of the pastors here.

6
00:01:07,340 --> 00:01:14,400
And we are excited to turn our attention to Advent this season as we prepare to celebrate Christmas.

7
00:01:14,400 --> 00:01:18,880
Now, if you didn't grow up in a church that celebrated Advent, that's okay.

8
00:01:18,880 --> 00:01:20,160
I didn't really either.

9
00:01:20,260 --> 00:01:25,400
But many churches throughout the world participate in a planned calendar.

10
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:27,720
I grew up in a Baptist church.

11
00:01:27,720 --> 00:01:28,380
We didn't do this.

12
00:01:28,380 --> 00:01:29,860
I didn't even really know this existed.

13
00:01:29,860 --> 00:01:42,200
I'm sure some of you grew up in churches that had regular calendars so that much of the church throughout the world can be focused on the same passages, the same themes all throughout the year.

14
00:01:42,260 --> 00:01:59,100
And although our church isn't a part of that tradition, I have come personally, and because I get to pick the calendar, you all have also come personally, corporately, to value two of the primary seasons in the church calendar, the season of Lent and the season of Advent.

15
00:01:59,100 --> 00:02:17,120
And the reason that these two seasons in particular grab my attention is because they focus on the central themes of the biblical narrative, the coming of Jesus, the death, burial, and resurrection of Jesus, and ultimately the promise of the coming again of Jesus.

16
00:02:17,120 --> 00:02:23,060
Lent is the season of preparation as we celebrate Christ's victory over death and the grave on Easter.

17
00:02:23,360 --> 00:02:28,060
And I don't know about you, but I feel like that just happened a month or two ago.

18
00:02:28,060 --> 00:02:29,880
But it did not.

19
00:02:29,880 --> 00:02:30,940
It happened a while ago.

20
00:02:30,940 --> 00:02:35,000
And again, the seasons remind us of how fast life can be.

21
00:02:35,000 --> 00:02:40,820
The seasons remind us of how God works in the midst of our lives, no matter how busy they might be.

22
00:02:40,820 --> 00:02:45,240
Lent is the season of preparation for the celebration of Christ's victory over death and the grave on Easter.

23
00:02:45,240 --> 00:02:47,220
Advent is the time that we reflect.

24
00:02:47,220 --> 00:02:52,580
We look back to the first coming of Jesus, the birth of Jesus.

25
00:02:52,580 --> 00:02:57,640
But ultimately, Advent isn't just about looking backwards to the first coming of Jesus.

26
00:02:57,640 --> 00:03:02,780
It's about looking forward with hope and with anticipation to the second coming of Jesus.

27
00:03:02,780 --> 00:03:12,520
We, as American Christians, I don't know about you, but think about the songs we sing, the things that we think about at Advent, tend to primarily focus on looking back to the birth of Jesus.

28
00:03:12,520 --> 00:03:14,240
But Advent is twofold.

29
00:03:14,240 --> 00:03:20,080
It is about looking back to the birth of Jesus and forward to the second coming of Jesus.

30
00:03:20,080 --> 00:03:24,080
Advent comes from a Latin word that simply means to come.

31
00:03:24,080 --> 00:03:27,020
I'm a scholar now.

32
00:03:27,020 --> 00:03:27,740
Did you guys know that?

33
00:03:27,740 --> 00:03:35,600
At Advent, we remind ourselves that God was faithful to fulfill many of his promises through the birth and life of Jesus.

34
00:03:35,600 --> 00:03:46,400
Therefore, because of that, because God was faithful to do what he promised to Israel to do through Jesus, we can be confident that what he has promised to do to the church in the future, he will do.

35
00:03:46,400 --> 00:03:52,920
We can have confidence that God will faithfully fulfill his future promises.

36
00:03:54,000 --> 00:03:59,220
Another beautiful thing about Advent that I've learned recently, and many of you probably already knew this.

37
00:03:59,220 --> 00:04:12,600
I hadn't put two and two together, but Advent was intentionally placed near the winter solstice, not because this is when the early church fathers thought that Jesus was born, but because it's the darkest time of the year.

38
00:04:13,600 --> 00:04:22,900
This intentionally reminds us that the world is not as it should be, that there is darkness where there was intended to be light.

39
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:33,260
But at Advent, we look back to Jesus, the light of the world, and we remind ourselves that in the midst of the darkest times, Jesus has brought forth his light.

40
00:04:33,260 --> 00:04:37,880
And because he did that in the past, we can be confident that he will do that in the future.

41
00:04:37,880 --> 00:04:48,460
One of my favorite promises for the future of Christ's coming is that the sun will be no more, that the presence of Christ will replace the sun, that he is the light of the world.

42
00:04:48,460 --> 00:04:53,500
We see it dimly now, but we will see it completely in the future.

43
00:04:53,500 --> 00:04:57,260
And that is what this Advent wreath is all about.

44
00:04:57,260 --> 00:05:08,440
At Advent, we light candles to remind ourselves that the light of the world has come, but that light is still dim, and we are looking forward to a time when we will see it completely and fully.

45
00:05:08,440 --> 00:05:09,960
And so I think I have a slide for this.

46
00:05:09,960 --> 00:05:13,700
My main theme for you all to think about throughout the season of Advent is this.

47
00:05:13,700 --> 00:05:18,360
At Advent, we look backwards with gratitude, and we look forward with anticipation.

48
00:05:18,360 --> 00:05:22,720
May we do both of those things this season, not just one or the other.

49
00:05:22,720 --> 00:05:30,400
May we look back to the coming of Jesus with gratitude and look forward to the second coming of Jesus with anticipation.

50
00:05:30,400 --> 00:05:37,520
And if you grew up in a church that celebrated Advent year over year over year, you know how it works, right?

51
00:05:37,520 --> 00:05:39,120
Hope, love, joy, peace.

52
00:05:39,120 --> 00:05:45,460
We look at these things, and we remind ourselves that Jesus has come, and he has brought elements of hope, love, joy, and peace.

53
00:05:45,460 --> 00:05:50,900
But ultimately, at his second coming, all of those things will be seen clearly and fully.

54
00:05:50,900 --> 00:06:04,600
And we talked about last week, the reason I think that sometimes participating in these regular rhythms of the church calendar is so important is because for Christians in the American church, we talked about this last week, we love to focus on learning new information.

55
00:06:06,020 --> 00:06:11,900
But I think oftentimes we just need to be reminded of the things that we already know to be true and put those things into practice.

56
00:06:11,900 --> 00:06:17,840
How often are we filling our mind with new information and not living in the information that we already know?

57
00:06:17,840 --> 00:06:28,160
Advent is about reminding ourselves that Christ is our hope, that Christ defines love for us, that he brings us joy and peace in the midst of chaotic circumstances.

58
00:06:28,160 --> 00:06:31,860
And not just information that we know, but information that we live into.

59
00:06:31,860 --> 00:06:44,780
So I think it's good that we remind ourselves of these things on a regular basis, not just so that we know them intellectually, but so that we hold ourselves accountable to live into them consistently.

60
00:06:44,780 --> 00:06:49,840
So today, we light the candle of hope.

61
00:06:49,840 --> 00:06:57,640
And last year, to be honest with you guys, I got some judgment from some of you for lighting the candles with a cigar torch.

62
00:06:57,640 --> 00:07:07,940
But those of you that judged me for lighting the candles with a cigar torch have never felt the anxiety of trying to click the Bic lighter five or six times and not get it to light.

63
00:07:07,940 --> 00:07:09,500
Like, you don't know the anxiety.

64
00:07:09,500 --> 00:07:16,800
So don't judge me for using my cigar torch because it will work the first time or the second.

65
00:07:16,800 --> 00:07:24,380
So today, we look, we focus our eyes on the hope that Jesus has brought.

66
00:07:24,380 --> 00:07:24,840
Let's pray.

67
00:07:26,700 --> 00:07:30,220
Father in heaven, we thank you.

68
00:07:30,220 --> 00:07:39,340
We thank you that we have the opportunity to set aside just a little bit of time in this chaotic season to look back to the coming of Jesus.

69
00:07:40,540 --> 00:07:47,140
The light of the world, who has brought us the first fruits of hope of the resurrection of Christ.

70
00:07:49,060 --> 00:07:56,260
God, today, may we get a glimpse of that hope, not just intellectually, but in our souls, in our bodies, in our whole selves.

71
00:07:56,260 --> 00:08:03,420
May we think about what this hope means for us in the midst of whatever difficult circumstances we might be facing.

72
00:08:03,420 --> 00:08:07,300
God, I know there are people in here that are holding on to their last strand of hope.

73
00:08:07,620 --> 00:08:11,180
May this morning you breathe a fresh wind of hope into their lives.

74
00:08:11,180 --> 00:08:28,240
God, and for those of us that haven't yet faced the difficult circumstances of life that will hit all of us from time to time, may we bury this hope deep down in our souls so that when that difficult season comes, we have something to cling to, something to root ourselves in.

75
00:08:28,240 --> 00:08:34,260
Lord, teach us to be a people of hope this morning, for your glory, for our good, and for the good of our neighbor.

76
00:08:34,260 --> 00:08:36,240
In Jesus' name I pray.

77
00:08:36,240 --> 00:08:37,440
Amen.

78
00:08:37,440 --> 00:08:42,040
If you want to follow along with me, we're going to be in Luke chapter 1 this morning.

79
00:08:42,040 --> 00:08:45,240
For most of our time, we'll hop over to 1 Corinthians 15.

80
00:08:45,240 --> 00:08:48,500
If you want to mark both places, then you'll know where to go.

81
00:08:48,500 --> 00:08:50,820
And if not, the words will be on the screen.

82
00:08:50,820 --> 00:08:52,960
I'd love for you to do me a favor.

83
00:08:52,960 --> 00:08:56,160
Raise your hand if you've ever met a famous person.

84
00:08:56,160 --> 00:08:57,760
Anybody in here met famous?

85
00:08:58,080 --> 00:08:58,760
Okay, a lot of us.

86
00:08:58,760 --> 00:09:00,360
Now, here's the kicker.

87
00:09:00,360 --> 00:09:06,140
Yell it out if you think the famous person you met is the most famous person represented in the room.

88
00:09:06,140 --> 00:09:11,480
Robert De Niro, Dan Aykroyd, George Bush.

89
00:09:11,480 --> 00:09:14,180
Those are three pretty good ones.

90
00:09:14,180 --> 00:09:15,160
Now, let's vote on who's...

91
00:09:15,160 --> 00:09:15,620
No, I'm just kidding.

92
00:09:15,620 --> 00:09:22,520
I think a sitting president probably has some significant celebrity power.

93
00:09:22,520 --> 00:09:25,860
I was trying to think about any famous people that I've met.

94
00:09:25,900 --> 00:09:28,500
I haven't really had any awesome encounters with some famous people.

95
00:09:28,500 --> 00:09:34,880
But a few weeks ago, our beloved worship leader and youth pastor, Donovan Russell, came back from California.

96
00:09:34,880 --> 00:09:43,320
And he told me about a fun encounter that he had with a, what I would call is a C-level famous person.

97
00:09:45,780 --> 00:09:51,760
Donovan ran into the actor that played the warden in Shawshank Redemption.

98
00:09:51,760 --> 00:09:52,940
I don't even know his name.

99
00:09:52,940 --> 00:09:54,420
That's how not famous he is to me.

100
00:09:54,420 --> 00:09:55,980
I love Shawshank Redemption.

101
00:09:55,980 --> 00:09:57,400
He's a fantastic actor.

102
00:09:57,400 --> 00:10:00,380
But, I mean, C-level at best, right?

103
00:10:01,040 --> 00:10:05,600
And so he's telling us this story about how his palms are getting sweaty, his knees are getting shaky.

104
00:10:05,600 --> 00:10:10,500
Emily's trying to talk him into not going up to this guy and making a fool out of himself.

105
00:10:10,500 --> 00:10:12,760
But, you know, you're in California.

106
00:10:12,760 --> 00:10:14,640
You get the opportunity to meet a famous person.

107
00:10:14,640 --> 00:10:17,960
And so Donovan makes his way to this guy outside the restaurant.

108
00:10:17,960 --> 00:10:22,080
And Donovan's like, you wouldn't believe how badly I embarrassed myself.

109
00:10:22,080 --> 00:10:23,980
You wouldn't believe it.

110
00:10:24,020 --> 00:10:24,900
And he recounts the story.

111
00:10:24,900 --> 00:10:30,440
And I'll let him, if you're really interested in the details of the story, be sure to hit Donovan up for that story.

112
00:10:30,440 --> 00:10:36,900
But the reason I share this is because I think many of us, no matter how famous the person that we met was,

113
00:10:36,900 --> 00:10:45,320
we felt this experience where we look at people who are, like, famous, and they just don't feel human to us.

114
00:10:45,320 --> 00:10:47,640
They feel totally different than us.

115
00:10:47,640 --> 00:10:53,440
And so it's hard to even relate to these people who have had these experiences of fame.

116
00:10:54,020 --> 00:11:02,060
The reason I share this story is because I think we have the tendency to do the same thing with famous biblical characters in familiar biblical stories.

117
00:11:02,060 --> 00:11:13,700
We tend to look at these people in the pages of Scripture and think of them as so other than human that we cannot even begin to relate to the experiences that they had.

118
00:11:15,380 --> 00:11:25,220
But my argument this morning is that the pages of Scripture are actually filled with God using real people with real lives to do amazing things through the power of the Holy Spirit.

119
00:11:25,220 --> 00:11:29,900
The people that we're reading about in the pages of Scripture are not superhuman or other than.

120
00:11:29,900 --> 00:11:33,800
They are regular people with difficult, challenging lives.

121
00:11:34,080 --> 00:11:39,580
And the Holy Spirit just happens to break into their story and invite them to participate in something incredible.

122
00:11:39,580 --> 00:11:48,060
And my point here for all of us this morning is that what if we're missing out on some of the things that God might want for us

123
00:11:48,060 --> 00:11:54,220
because we've read these pages of Scripture in such a way that we cannot relate to the stories that we're dealing with?

124
00:11:54,900 --> 00:12:02,020
What if, like many of the characters in the Bible, God wants to use us to do things that we can never even imagine?

125
00:12:02,020 --> 00:12:07,000
This morning I want to look at a familiar Christmas passage with the famous biblical character

126
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:13,240
and see if we can't relate to what she experienced in this story, specifically around the idea of hope.

127
00:12:13,240 --> 00:12:19,820
So, Luke chapter 1, starting in verse 26, most of us will probably be familiar with this even if we didn't grow up in church.

128
00:12:19,820 --> 00:12:20,560
It says this,

129
00:12:20,560 --> 00:12:27,640
In the sixth month of Elizabeth's pregnancy, God sent the angel Gabriel to Nazareth, a village in Galilee.

130
00:12:27,640 --> 00:12:32,600
A village, save that for later, to a virgin named Mary.

131
00:12:32,600 --> 00:12:37,120
She was engaged to be married to a man named Joseph, a descendant of King David.

132
00:12:37,120 --> 00:12:39,480
Gabriel appeared to her and he said,

133
00:12:39,480 --> 00:12:41,200
Greetings, favored woman.

134
00:12:41,200 --> 00:12:44,120
The Lord is with you.

135
00:12:44,120 --> 00:12:49,480
Confused and disturbed, Mary tried to think what the angel could mean.

136
00:12:49,820 --> 00:12:54,640
Don't be afraid, Mary, the angel told her, for you have found favor with God.

137
00:12:54,640 --> 00:12:59,220
You will conceive and give birth to a son and you will name him Jesus.

138
00:12:59,220 --> 00:13:03,960
He will be very great and he will be called the Son of the Most High.

139
00:13:03,960 --> 00:13:12,920
The Lord will give him the throne of his ancestor David and he will reign over Israel forever and his kingdom will never end.

140
00:13:14,560 --> 00:13:18,040
Mary asked the angel, but how could this happen?

141
00:13:18,040 --> 00:13:21,220
I am a virgin.

142
00:13:21,220 --> 00:13:30,400
As I reread this passage this week for probably the 200th time, something jumped off the page at me.

143
00:13:31,120 --> 00:13:39,260
What jumped off the page at me, what I never noticed before and probably should have noticed because I've been taught how to read the Bible and yet sometimes I miss it.

144
00:13:41,140 --> 00:13:50,460
Is that Mary took issue with what the angel said, not because she didn't think God was faithful to fulfill his promises to the nation of Israel.

145
00:13:50,460 --> 00:13:56,320
She very easily could have said, look around at the circumstances of the nation of Israel.

146
00:13:56,320 --> 00:14:03,160
There is no way, shape or form that God is going to actually fulfill the promises that he promised to our ancestors.

147
00:14:04,020 --> 00:14:06,220
But that's not the thing she took issue with.

148
00:14:06,220 --> 00:14:09,500
She didn't take issue with God being faithful to fulfill his promises.

149
00:14:09,500 --> 00:14:19,880
It's almost assumed in her response to the angel that she has a confident expectation that God is going to do the thing that he promised to do for the nation of Israel.

150
00:14:19,880 --> 00:14:25,280
She's just not so sure about how she might be participating in this process.

151
00:14:26,100 --> 00:14:28,600
Notice she doesn't say, look around.

152
00:14:28,600 --> 00:14:30,240
God's not going to send his Messiah.

153
00:14:30,240 --> 00:14:31,520
He's rejected us.

154
00:14:31,520 --> 00:14:34,140
Look at all of our circumstances.

155
00:14:34,140 --> 00:14:35,540
God's not going to send his Messiah.

156
00:14:35,540 --> 00:14:38,220
She doesn't say, there's no way he's going to send his Messiah.

157
00:14:38,220 --> 00:14:39,880
She says, how could this be?

158
00:14:39,880 --> 00:14:41,260
I'm a virgin.

159
00:14:41,260 --> 00:14:43,340
This isn't going to work scientifically.

160
00:14:43,340 --> 00:14:49,860
I might not be the smartest, most influential person in the world, but I know how biology works.

161
00:14:49,860 --> 00:14:50,960
I am a virgin.

162
00:14:54,280 --> 00:15:00,920
And in our culture, the word hope has come to mean something totally different than what it meant to the people of Israel.

163
00:15:00,920 --> 00:15:02,720
You guys have all probably heard this before.

164
00:15:02,720 --> 00:15:13,320
Actually, all four of the words that we're going to talk about throughout the Advent have come to mean something different to us in our culture than they meant to the biblical audience.

165
00:15:13,320 --> 00:15:18,380
In our culture, hope is a word used to describe wishful thinking towards things that are outside of our control.

166
00:15:18,380 --> 00:15:21,720
I hope that it snows on Christmas.

167
00:15:21,720 --> 00:15:23,240
Anybody else have that same hope?

168
00:15:24,280 --> 00:15:25,420
Some of you are like, no way.

169
00:15:25,420 --> 00:15:28,980
I don't want to be shoveling snow when I should be open in presents.

170
00:15:28,980 --> 00:15:32,700
I hope that the commanders make it to the Super Bowl.

171
00:15:32,700 --> 00:15:34,020
Anybody else have that hope?

172
00:15:34,020 --> 00:15:41,900
Well, a three-game losing streak might start to have some of that hope dwindle away.

173
00:15:42,660 --> 00:15:54,900
We have this wishful thinking towards things outside of our control, but hope in the Bible is usually not referring to this type of hope, but more of a confident expectation rooted in the character and promises of God.

174
00:15:54,980 --> 00:16:01,000
Hebrews 10, 23 says this, let us hold tightly without wavering to the hope that we affirm.

175
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,260
What is our hope rooted in?

176
00:16:03,260 --> 00:16:06,580
God can be trusted to keep his promise.

177
00:16:07,440 --> 00:16:12,000
This is biblical hope, a confident expectation in the promises of God.

178
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:25,320
Romans 4 says this, even when there was no reason for hope, no circumstantial evidence to hope, Abraham, one of the fathers of the nation of Israel, kept hoping, believing that he would become the father of many nations.

179
00:16:26,140 --> 00:16:26,620
Why?

180
00:16:26,620 --> 00:16:29,440
Why did he keep hoping when there was no reason to hope?

181
00:16:29,440 --> 00:16:32,420
He kept hoping because of what God had said to him.

182
00:16:32,420 --> 00:16:36,100
Abraham never wavered in believing God's promise.

183
00:16:36,100 --> 00:16:40,220
He was fully convinced that God can do whatever he promises.

184
00:16:40,220 --> 00:16:48,000
This is biblical hope, a confident expectation that God will keep his promises no matter what our circumstances look like.

185
00:16:48,500 --> 00:16:58,020
And what I would argue from this passage this morning is that I think that Mary very much so had a confident biblical hope in the coming promise of the Messiah.

186
00:16:58,020 --> 00:17:03,280
She doesn't say there's no way the Messiah is going to come.

187
00:17:03,280 --> 00:17:04,500
Look at my circumstances.

188
00:17:04,500 --> 00:17:08,660
She just says, how might I participate in this?

189
00:17:09,600 --> 00:17:22,160
And what I want to do is just imagine for a few minutes the circumstances around Mary's life that could have led her to lose all confident expectation in the promises of God.

190
00:17:22,160 --> 00:17:26,700
Mary is a young Jewish girl, very young, likely.

191
00:17:26,700 --> 00:17:29,080
Many scholars think she's 12 or 13 years old.

192
00:17:29,080 --> 00:17:35,980
A young Jewish girl born into a poor family, living in a podunk town in the middle of nowhere.

193
00:17:36,920 --> 00:17:38,980
A small village called Nazareth.

194
00:17:38,980 --> 00:17:44,100
Later in the Scriptures, someone will ask this question when they're told that Jesus is from Nazareth.

195
00:17:44,100 --> 00:17:44,760
They'll say this.

196
00:17:44,760 --> 00:17:54,960
If you want to know what the culture thought about the place that Mary came from, this poor young girl with challenges all around her, they said, can anything good come from Nazareth?

197
00:17:54,960 --> 00:17:57,860
This is the culture.

198
00:17:57,860 --> 00:18:00,280
This is the experience of Mary.

199
00:18:00,280 --> 00:18:05,720
Not only is she a young, poor girl living in a podunk town in the middle of nowhere.

200
00:18:05,880 --> 00:18:09,460
Mary's people have been crushed under the weight of Roman oppression.

201
00:18:09,460 --> 00:18:16,140
They're essentially at this point living in mandatory slavery with a tax rate of around 80 to 90%.

202
00:18:16,140 --> 00:18:18,520
And on top of all of that, oh yeah.

203
00:18:18,520 --> 00:18:22,640
And remember, remember later in the story, it'll say that they're going to Bethlehem.

204
00:18:22,640 --> 00:18:22,840
Why?

205
00:18:22,840 --> 00:18:25,080
Because Augustus had a Caesar.

206
00:18:25,080 --> 00:18:28,260
Augustus had a, somebody help me.

207
00:18:28,260 --> 00:18:29,420
Census.

208
00:18:29,420 --> 00:18:30,120
Thank you.

209
00:18:30,560 --> 00:18:39,820
He was having a census to figure out how many people were around so that he could probably raise the tax rate.

210
00:18:39,820 --> 00:18:40,100
Okay.

211
00:18:40,100 --> 00:18:43,660
So, I mean, Mary's circumstances all around us.

212
00:18:43,660 --> 00:18:51,740
On top of that, the God who promised to rescue Israel from their oppressors has seemingly been absent from the story of Israel for hundreds of years.

213
00:18:53,020 --> 00:19:03,940
This is the lived experience of Mary, poor, destitute, hopeless for all circumstances around her.

214
00:19:03,940 --> 00:19:11,200
And yet, when the angel shows up and says, the God who promised to send his Messiah is doing it now, she doesn't say there's no way.

215
00:19:11,660 --> 00:19:13,620
She just says, but I'm a virgin.

216
00:19:13,620 --> 00:19:15,180
Isn't that beautiful?

217
00:19:15,180 --> 00:19:17,960
That jumped off the pages of Scripture to me this week.

218
00:19:17,960 --> 00:19:25,160
She had a confident expectation that God would send a Messiah, even though her circumstances would have told her that none of that is true.

219
00:19:25,160 --> 00:19:28,400
She was holding on to a biblical hope in the promises of God.

220
00:19:28,400 --> 00:19:32,540
She just wasn't sure how she might play a part in that role.

221
00:19:35,920 --> 00:19:44,020
With all of the circumstantial evidence stacked against her, she was still seemingly holding on to a confident expectation that God would do what he promised.

222
00:19:44,020 --> 00:19:58,500
I don't think it would be a big leap for me to say that the circumstances Mary is facing is likely as bad, if not worse, than pretty much all of us in this room.

223
00:19:58,500 --> 00:20:02,280
However, I do not want to downplay the suffering of the people in this room.

224
00:20:02,280 --> 00:20:07,960
I know many of you, and I've had many conversations with you, those of you who are suffering right now.

225
00:20:07,960 --> 00:20:17,860
Many of us are suffering, and the holiday season tends to exasperate that suffering, magnifies that emotion.

226
00:20:17,860 --> 00:20:22,700
Many of our worldly hopes have been let down over and over and over again.

227
00:20:22,700 --> 00:20:25,480
The people we expected to stay have left.

228
00:20:25,480 --> 00:20:29,220
The money we expected to have in our account has dwindled.

229
00:20:29,600 --> 00:20:34,060
The regular checkup at the doctor's office turned into an unexpected diagnosis.

230
00:20:34,060 --> 00:20:43,160
Even our hope, even our hope in God to deliver us from the pain is fading away.

231
00:20:43,160 --> 00:20:46,240
Many of us have years and years of unanswered prayers.

232
00:20:49,020 --> 00:20:53,220
These are the experiences of the people of God on a regular basis.

233
00:20:53,220 --> 00:20:58,240
If you can resonate at all with any of Mary's circumstances, you are not alone.

234
00:20:58,240 --> 00:21:05,180
This was her experience, and yet somehow she was holding on to the hope that God would do what he said.

235
00:21:05,180 --> 00:21:10,240
How could Mary possibly have hope in the promises of God in light of her circumstances?

236
00:21:11,420 --> 00:21:20,580
Well, to go back to our illustration of the church calendar, the Jewish people had one of the best religious calendars in all of human history.

237
00:21:20,580 --> 00:21:26,940
Their calendar, their regular weekly, monthly, and yearly rhythms were built around reminding themselves,

238
00:21:26,940 --> 00:21:31,120
regularly reminding themselves of God's faithfulness to their ancestors in the past.

239
00:21:31,700 --> 00:21:41,840
From Noah to Abraham to Moses to David, and on and on we could go, they were constantly looking backward to the promises that God fulfilled through his people.

240
00:21:41,840 --> 00:21:48,080
And they were constantly looking forward to the promises of the Messiah who was to come from the line of David.

241
00:21:48,080 --> 00:21:49,800
This was likely Mary's fuel.

242
00:21:50,060 --> 00:21:58,220
The fuel for Mary's hope was her participation in the Jewish calendar, reminding herself of what God had done for her people in the past,

243
00:21:58,220 --> 00:22:02,280
and looking forward to the promises that he made to them in the future.

244
00:22:02,280 --> 00:22:10,720
And this can become the same fuel for us to experience the type of hope that goes beyond any circumstantial evidence.

245
00:22:10,720 --> 00:22:15,920
With one major, major change.

246
00:22:18,420 --> 00:22:23,400
See, Mary only had Old Testament fulfillments to look back to.

247
00:22:23,400 --> 00:22:30,060
We have the evidence of the empty tomb to look back to.

248
00:22:30,060 --> 00:22:35,600
We live in a privileged position because the things that Mary looked back to in the Jewish experience,

249
00:22:35,600 --> 00:22:41,260
the promises that God fulfilled through Moses and David and all of the ancestors of the faith,

250
00:22:41,260 --> 00:22:45,320
those things were all shadows of the thing to come in Jesus.

251
00:22:45,580 --> 00:22:51,940
We live in a privileged position because the things Mary looked back to were all shadows pointing to God's ultimate redemption plan of Jesus.

252
00:22:51,940 --> 00:22:56,020
And now we get to live in a position where we look back to an empty tomb,

253
00:22:56,020 --> 00:23:00,380
and that gives us confident expectation no matter what our circumstances might be.

254
00:23:00,380 --> 00:23:04,860
We can now look back to the death and resurrection of Jesus,

255
00:23:04,860 --> 00:23:09,280
and because of that confident expectation, look forward to the promises of the second coming.

256
00:23:10,280 --> 00:23:12,820
1 Corinthians 15.

257
00:23:12,820 --> 00:23:13,880
I've got to go fast.

258
00:23:13,880 --> 00:23:17,760
I never think I'm going to have to go as fast as I'm going to have to go until we get here.

259
00:23:17,760 --> 00:23:18,140
Okay.

260
00:23:18,140 --> 00:23:20,740
You guys need to listen faster, okay?

261
00:23:20,740 --> 00:23:22,880
Let's move it on.

262
00:23:22,880 --> 00:23:23,400
Move it on.

263
00:23:23,400 --> 00:23:24,080
All right.

264
00:23:24,080 --> 00:23:25,440
Verse 16.

265
00:23:25,440 --> 00:23:30,780
This is one of the most clear teachings of hope in the New Testament.

266
00:23:30,940 --> 00:23:36,000
And if there is no resurrection of the dead, then Christ has not been raised.

267
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:43,160
And if Christ has not been raised, if there's no empty tomb, then your faith, this is depressing, but it's the facts.

268
00:23:43,160 --> 00:23:47,880
If Christ has not been raised, your faith is useless and you are still guilty of your sins.

269
00:23:47,880 --> 00:23:52,460
In that case, all who have died believing in Jesus are lost.

270
00:23:53,120 --> 00:23:58,380
And if our hope in Christ is only for this life, we are more to be pitied than anyone in the world.

271
00:23:58,380 --> 00:24:05,020
If you're wasting an hour of your time on a Sunday morning to come in here and sing about a guy who didn't raise from the dead,

272
00:24:05,020 --> 00:24:08,480
you are to be pitied more than anyone else.

273
00:24:08,480 --> 00:24:13,800
But in fact, Christ has been raised from the dead.

274
00:24:13,800 --> 00:24:18,260
He is the first of a great harvest of all who have died.

275
00:24:19,100 --> 00:24:26,080
In one of the New Testament's most in-depth teachings on hope, Paul reminds us that our hope as Christians is rooted in the resurrection of Jesus.

276
00:24:26,080 --> 00:24:34,540
If Jesus hasn't been raised, all we have is wishful thinking about things outside of our control.

277
00:24:34,540 --> 00:24:42,000
That's why people obsess over rooting for bad sports teams.

278
00:24:43,140 --> 00:24:47,760
Because in the difficulty of all of the things they have going on in their life,

279
00:24:47,760 --> 00:24:50,800
they're holding on to hope that the commanders are going to go to the Super Bowl,

280
00:24:50,800 --> 00:24:53,480
because then maybe they'll have a little bit of joy in their life.

281
00:24:53,480 --> 00:25:00,380
If Christ hasn't been raised, all we have is wishful thinking on things outside of our control.

282
00:25:00,380 --> 00:25:07,720
But if Christ has been raised, we have confident expectation that God will do what he said he will do.

283
00:25:08,100 --> 00:25:11,360
Jesus will return and restore all the brokenness of creation.

284
00:25:11,360 --> 00:25:13,440
He will wipe away every tear from our eyes.

285
00:25:13,440 --> 00:25:16,680
Weapons of war will be turned into weapons of farming.

286
00:25:16,680 --> 00:25:23,480
And the earth and all who have bowed their knees to the Prince of Peace will experience the ultimate fulfillment of our hope.

287
00:25:25,360 --> 00:25:30,460
This is why the Christian hope goes beyond any experiential thing that we face.

288
00:25:30,460 --> 00:25:32,300
All of our experiences.

289
00:25:32,300 --> 00:25:34,800
Our hope goes beyond those experiences.

290
00:25:34,800 --> 00:25:38,780
Just like Mary, beyond her experiences of the difficulty of life.

291
00:25:38,780 --> 00:25:40,300
She had a hope.

292
00:25:40,300 --> 00:25:43,660
Because her hope was not rooted in her circumstances.

293
00:25:43,660 --> 00:25:47,000
Her hope was rooted in the character and promises of God.

294
00:25:50,760 --> 00:25:55,960
Hope is the confident expectation that ultimate good will come through the promises of God.

295
00:25:55,960 --> 00:26:04,160
But the thing that I was learning this week is that hope doesn't stop with just confident expectation and the promises of God.

296
00:26:04,160 --> 00:26:12,860
I think God is actually inviting us into not just the confident expectation, but actually participation in the promises of God.

297
00:26:12,860 --> 00:26:16,680
See, Mary had hope that God would send his Messiah.

298
00:26:16,680 --> 00:26:19,800
She just wasn't sure how she fit into the story.

299
00:26:19,940 --> 00:26:22,380
Mary asked the angel, how can this happen?

300
00:26:22,380 --> 00:26:24,700
Because I am a virgin.

301
00:26:24,700 --> 00:26:28,260
A couple things about Mary here in this story that humanizes her.

302
00:26:28,260 --> 00:26:31,440
That makes her a little bit less, or a little bit more relatable.

303
00:26:31,440 --> 00:26:36,260
Mary lacked some crucial biblical knowledge about the promises of God.

304
00:26:36,260 --> 00:26:40,280
Although she's clinging on to the hope that the Messiah would come.

305
00:26:40,280 --> 00:26:46,300
Isaiah 7, 14 clearly says that therefore the Lord himself will give you a sign.

306
00:26:46,420 --> 00:26:51,640
The virgin will conceive and give birth to a son and will call him Emmanuel.

307
00:26:52,800 --> 00:26:53,300
It's the prophecy of God.

308
00:26:53,300 --> 00:26:54,440
It's the prophecy of God.

309
00:26:54,440 --> 00:26:58,580
And so what I want to point out here is this isn't to shame Mary.

310
00:26:58,580 --> 00:27:01,980
She lived in a culture where women were not educated to the level of men.

311
00:27:01,980 --> 00:27:06,020
She didn't have the opportunity to interact with the specifics of the biblical text.

312
00:27:06,020 --> 00:27:07,360
She had the big picture.

313
00:27:07,360 --> 00:27:15,660
She knew how God had been faithful in the past, but she didn't have the specific biblical knowledge that would allow her to have confidence when angel Gabriel shows up and says,

314
00:27:15,740 --> 00:27:17,560
You're going to birth the Messiah.

315
00:27:17,560 --> 00:27:28,400
And I think what this story points out to us is that God is in the business of using people, not because they've got all the answers, but because they can trust that he does.

316
00:27:28,400 --> 00:27:36,140
And I think many of us in this room, we opt out of the ministry that God is calling us to do because we don't feel qualified.

317
00:27:37,120 --> 00:27:38,680
We don't feel like we know enough.

318
00:27:38,680 --> 00:27:46,800
We don't feel like we have enough of the right information or the right resources to be participants in what God wants to do in and around us.

319
00:27:46,800 --> 00:27:50,020
This is the position that Mary found herself in.

320
00:27:50,020 --> 00:27:50,820
She was confident.

321
00:27:50,820 --> 00:27:54,640
She had a faith and a belief that God was going to do what he said he was going to do,

322
00:27:54,640 --> 00:28:00,140
but she didn't have the knowledge that she needed to know that she could still participate in this.

323
00:28:00,140 --> 00:28:03,820
I was thinking about this this weekend.

324
00:28:05,580 --> 00:28:10,700
You guys, I don't know if I should tell you this or not, but I'm going to because we started it.

325
00:28:10,700 --> 00:28:18,740
So there are multiple people in this congregation who have significantly higher and better biblical education than I do.

326
00:28:18,740 --> 00:28:32,460
And yet week after week, God continues to ask me to come up here and talk about the Bible in front of a number of you who have much higher credentials in biblical education than I do.

327
00:28:33,200 --> 00:28:40,220
Now, what would happen if God, who called me, I have a calling on my life to come up here and to preach God's word.

328
00:28:40,220 --> 00:28:42,860
But what if I had this thought creep into the back of my mind?

329
00:28:42,860 --> 00:28:45,880
I don't know as much as a lot of people that I'm called to preach to.

330
00:28:45,880 --> 00:28:52,360
Well, what would happen is you guys would just find another pastor and everything would be all right.

331
00:28:52,360 --> 00:29:04,680
But I would miss out on doing the thing that God invited me to do because I had doubt about my qualifications or my education.

332
00:29:06,280 --> 00:29:14,960
What I think God wants me to share with you all this morning is that God isn't looking for people who know it all, but for people who are willing to serve and trust that he will provide.

333
00:29:16,180 --> 00:29:22,420
What ministry opportunities might you be missing out on because you think you need to learn more before you do what God's calling you to do?

334
00:29:24,340 --> 00:29:29,500
Not only does she lack a little bit of biblical knowledge, but she lacks confidence in her participation.

335
00:29:29,500 --> 00:29:32,860
Again, Mary seems to be confident that the Messiah will come.

336
00:29:32,860 --> 00:29:36,900
She just isn't sure how she fits into the plan.

337
00:29:38,160 --> 00:29:40,740
I think many of us are in a similar position.

338
00:29:40,740 --> 00:29:51,220
We believe that God can and will do what he promised to do, but we lack the confidence that we have a part to play in God's eternal kingdom coming to earth as it is in heaven.

339
00:29:51,220 --> 00:29:58,380
My big aha moment for this week, every once in a while, usually every week I have some kind of aha moment.

340
00:29:58,380 --> 00:30:02,820
Sometimes it's not until Friday or Saturday, which makes my week a little bit stressful.

341
00:30:04,460 --> 00:30:23,780
But I had this aha moment early in the week that hope is not just a confident expectation, but it is an intentional participation in the promises of God.

342
00:30:23,780 --> 00:30:25,540
Let's continue reading in verse 35.

343
00:30:25,540 --> 00:30:33,020
After Mary asks her very humble and reasonable question, how am I going to birth the Messiah?

344
00:30:33,160 --> 00:30:33,960
I'm a virgin.

345
00:30:33,960 --> 00:30:41,680
The angel replied, the Holy Spirit will come upon you and the power of the Most High will overshadow you.

346
00:30:41,680 --> 00:30:46,160
So the baby to be born will be holy and he will be called the Son of God.

347
00:30:46,160 --> 00:30:50,280
What's more, your relative Elizabeth has become pregnant in her old age.

348
00:30:50,280 --> 00:30:57,060
People used to say that she was barren, but she has conceived a son and is now in her sixth month.

349
00:30:57,060 --> 00:31:02,960
Listen to this, verse 37, for the word of God will never fail.

350
00:31:02,960 --> 00:31:10,320
Mary responded, I am the Lord's servant.

351
00:31:10,320 --> 00:31:14,820
May everything you have said about me come true.

352
00:31:16,800 --> 00:31:23,440
Mary had one humble, reasonable question.

353
00:31:23,440 --> 00:31:27,280
And the Lord is kind and gracious to answer that question.

354
00:31:27,280 --> 00:31:29,120
He says, the Holy Spirit's going to come on you.

355
00:31:29,120 --> 00:31:34,440
He will conceive and you will give birth as a virgin.

356
00:31:34,440 --> 00:31:38,500
And what I want to kind of start to wrap the plane with, Marissa, you can come up.

357
00:31:39,760 --> 00:31:40,960
I did it again.

358
00:31:40,960 --> 00:31:43,920
I'm going to have to.

359
00:31:43,920 --> 00:31:45,480
All right.

360
00:31:45,480 --> 00:31:51,460
Mary is a willing participant in the promises of God, no matter the cost.

361
00:31:53,220 --> 00:32:01,240
After she gets her questions answered, after she has this brief, humble, reasonable interaction with the angel,

362
00:32:01,240 --> 00:32:06,240
she decides that she's going to be a willing participant in the promises of God, no matter the cost.

363
00:32:06,240 --> 00:32:18,460
She would have to have, think about, this isn't just as easy as saying like, okay, I will be the Lord's servant.

364
00:32:19,180 --> 00:32:25,300
Do to me whatever you want to do, and life is good up and to the right for Mary from now on.

365
00:32:25,300 --> 00:32:30,460
Think about the difficult circumstances that Mary's going to have to face moving forward.

366
00:32:30,460 --> 00:32:37,280
She'd have to have some awkward conversations with Joseph, the person that she's legally engaged to be married to.

367
00:32:37,280 --> 00:32:40,540
Engagement's slightly different in that culture than in our culture.

368
00:32:40,540 --> 00:32:46,780
There was a contract that has been signed, and that contract was contingent on her remaining a virgin until the point that she was married.

369
00:32:48,280 --> 00:32:53,080
Going to Joseph and Joseph's parents and convincing them that, no, I am still a virgin.

370
00:32:53,080 --> 00:32:55,340
God just decided to put his child in me.

371
00:32:55,340 --> 00:33:01,680
Slightly awkward, difficult, challenging circumstances.

372
00:33:01,680 --> 00:33:08,080
Not only that, she'd likely experience, think about living in a podunk town in the middle of nowhere with not a whole lot going on.

373
00:33:08,080 --> 00:33:13,300
She'd likely experience the shame-filled gossip of people that she thought were her friends.

374
00:33:15,100 --> 00:33:17,820
Anybody in here had that experience?

375
00:33:17,820 --> 00:33:27,940
Not to mention the cost of, in her mind at this moment, assuming Joseph's going to leave her, she's saying yes to raising a child on her own.

376
00:33:27,940 --> 00:33:32,060
A poor girl from a farm village in the middle of nowhere.

377
00:33:35,660 --> 00:33:36,300
Mary's a willing participant in the promises of God.

378
00:33:36,300 --> 00:33:39,100
Mary's a willing participant in the promises of God.

379
00:33:39,100 --> 00:33:47,420
Her confident expectation in the character and promises of God led her to be willing, to be obedient to this difficult invitation of God.

380
00:33:47,420 --> 00:33:51,380
Anybody ever, anybody in here ever got a difficult invitation from God?

381
00:33:55,580 --> 00:33:59,260
God's typically not in the business of inviting us to do something that's easy.

382
00:33:59,260 --> 00:34:08,940
And yet, because of her confident expectation in the promises and character of God, she was a willing participant.

383
00:34:18,620 --> 00:34:27,980
So I said, I hoped by the end of this that you would see Mary as a slightly more relatable character than just this famous person from a familiar passage.

384
00:34:31,340 --> 00:34:35,460
You, like Mary, have been offered the presence of the Holy Spirit.

385
00:34:35,460 --> 00:34:48,260
The same Spirit that rose Jesus from the grave, the same Spirit that conceived Jesus in Mary's virgin womb, is living inside of each of us who have trusted in Christ.

386
00:34:49,700 --> 00:34:55,320
Like Mary, we have been invited to participate in God's plan of bringing heaven to earth.

387
00:34:55,320 --> 00:35:09,820
Ephesians 2, verse 10 says that you are God's workmanship, created in Christ Jesus to do good works, which he prepared in advance for me to do, us to do, you to do.

388
00:35:09,820 --> 00:35:19,680
Like Mary, we've been offered the Holy Spirit and invited to participate in the promises of God.

389
00:35:19,700 --> 00:35:30,620
Like Mary, many of us in this room are experiencing difficult circumstances that might cause us to lose hope in God altogether.

390
00:35:30,620 --> 00:35:40,440
That's you, if you're in here and you're just clinging on to your last strand of hope.

391
00:35:44,960 --> 00:35:46,200
I empathize with you.

392
00:35:46,200 --> 00:35:51,940
And I really don't have, from stage, much to give you.

393
00:35:51,940 --> 00:36:03,760
We could sit down, we could have a face-to-face conversation with some more nuance, but ultimately, as a pastor, all I have to give you is what Paul gave the Corinthians, and that is the vision of the empty tomb.

394
00:36:06,080 --> 00:36:19,500
If Jesus truly raised from the dead, then no matter your earthly circumstances, and whether or not God answers the prayers that you've been praying for years and years and years or not, you can have a hope that goes beyond this life into eternity.

395
00:36:21,400 --> 00:36:26,800
My wife came back from a conference, and she got a hat, cool merch.

396
00:36:26,800 --> 00:36:28,820
Can't go to a conference without cool merch.

397
00:36:28,820 --> 00:36:34,740
The hat's been hanging in our bedroom, and I walk by it every once in a while, and the hat says,

398
00:36:34,880 --> 00:36:37,180
If it's not good, God's not done.

399
00:36:37,180 --> 00:36:48,780
If you're in here, and you're struggling with everything inside of you to hold on to hope, if it's not good, God's not done.

400
00:36:48,780 --> 00:36:51,640
And that might not mean this earth.

401
00:36:51,640 --> 00:36:56,720
And that's where we've got to be real in our preaching, authentic in our preaching.

402
00:36:56,720 --> 00:37:04,060
I hear a lot of pastors who talk about, if it's not good, God's not done, and he's promising you that God's going to show up in your circumstance and fix everything.

403
00:37:04,060 --> 00:37:09,880
That might not be the case, this side of death.

404
00:37:09,880 --> 00:37:16,840
But it will be the case when Christ returns.

405
00:37:16,840 --> 00:37:25,980
That passage in 1 Corinthians 15, another thing that stuck out to me this week, talked about Christ being the first fruits of the resurrection.

406
00:37:27,200 --> 00:37:28,140
I'm not a farmer.

407
00:37:28,140 --> 00:37:28,960
Some of you are.

408
00:37:28,960 --> 00:37:32,100
You know the experience of the first fruit.

409
00:37:32,100 --> 00:37:33,980
And I was thinking about that.

410
00:37:33,980 --> 00:37:36,540
Again, not a farmer.

411
00:37:36,540 --> 00:37:38,300
So if I butcher this, I'm really sorry.

412
00:37:38,300 --> 00:37:42,260
I asked some farming friends, and they seemed to confirm it.

413
00:37:42,260 --> 00:37:49,460
But my understanding is, you plant a seed in the ground, and the seed has to die for the plant to grow.

414
00:37:49,460 --> 00:37:56,380
Jesus went into the tomb dead as a doornail.

415
00:37:56,720 --> 00:37:59,640
Not kind of alive, not in a coma, dead.

416
00:37:59,640 --> 00:38:14,940
He rose again three days later to prove that he was who he said he was, and that we can have a confident expectation in him to fulfill his future promises, no matter the circumstances that we might face.

417
00:38:15,320 --> 00:38:29,140
So if you're in here and you're struggling to hold on to hope, like Mary looked back to the promises of God from Moses to David and onward, we can look back to the empty tomb and remind ourselves that if it's not good, God's not done.

418
00:38:31,080 --> 00:38:36,020
Like Mary, we might feel the lack of confidence to be used by God.

419
00:38:36,020 --> 00:38:41,560
But like Mary, we've been offered the power of the Holy Spirit.

420
00:38:41,560 --> 00:38:45,800
We've been invited to participate in the promises of God.

421
00:38:49,060 --> 00:38:51,580
So will we respond like Mary?

422
00:38:51,580 --> 00:38:54,720
Let it be done to me as you have said.

423
00:38:54,720 --> 00:38:56,560
I am the Lord's servant.

424
00:38:56,560 --> 00:39:07,180
Will we step into confident expectation of God to use us to do exceedingly abundantly more than we could ever ask, think, or imagine?

425
00:39:07,180 --> 00:39:13,320
Or will we sit it out on the sidelines and let other people participate in what God wants us to do?

426
00:39:13,320 --> 00:39:26,660
Hope is the confident expectation and intentional participation in the promises of God until he returns.

427
00:39:26,660 --> 00:39:27,800
Let's pray.

428
00:39:30,260 --> 00:39:38,320
Father, God, I know there are a number of us that are all over the spectrum when it comes to biblical hope.

429
00:39:38,320 --> 00:39:43,960
I pray your Holy Spirit today would speak truth to each one of us.

430
00:39:43,960 --> 00:39:46,440
Let us hear what we need to hear.

431
00:39:46,440 --> 00:39:51,780
Let us believe in what we need to believe in and let us have perseverance.

432
00:39:51,780 --> 00:39:58,360
Let us be encouraged by the story of Mary, a poor farm girl from the middle of nowhere.

433
00:40:00,260 --> 00:40:29,320
God, may we see her story in her story of faith that we can learn from, that we can experience ourself as we receive the gift of the Holy Spirit and say yes to the invitation of God in our lives.

434
00:40:30,260 --> 00:40:38,340
God, may we be a church that gets to see you do incredible things through ordinary people just saying yes to whatever it is you're asking them to do.

435
00:40:38,340 --> 00:40:44,460
It's in Jesus' name I pray.

436
00:40:44,460 --> 00:40:46,420
Amen.

437
00:40:46,420 --> 00:40:50,840
I'd love for you to stand up as we dismiss.

438
00:40:50,840 --> 00:40:54,720
I'm going to read a prayer of hope over each one of us.

439
00:40:59,860 --> 00:41:11,520
Church, may the God of hope fill you with all joy and peace as you trust in him so that you may overflow with hope by the power of the Holy Spirit.

440
00:41:11,520 --> 00:41:12,860
Amen.

441
00:41:12,860 --> 00:41:15,320
Go in peace.

442
00:41:15,320 --> 00:41:16,280
Thank you.

443
00:41:16,280 --> 00:41:16,340
Thank you.

444
00:41:29,460 --> 00:41:59,440
Thank you.
